#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# erfi

#% text
# `erfi(x)` gives the imaginary error function `-im*erf(im*x)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `erfi(x)` is `2/sqrt(pi)` times the integral of `exp(t^2)` for `t` from 0 to `x` (DLMF 7.2.1 continued to the imaginary axis). It is odd and grows like `exp(x^2)`.

#% item
# `erfi(0)` is 0; other exact arguments stay unevaluated. Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values.

#% item
# `erfi` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
erfi(1.0)
#% output
#= 1.6504257587975428

#% cell
erfi(0)
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
erfi(-0.5)
#% output
#= -0.614952094696511

#% cell
n(erfi(1), 30)
#% output
#= 1.650425758797542876025337729561362

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Large arguments:

#% cell
erfi(10.0)
#% output
#= 1.5243074227086696e42

#% text
# A complex argument:

#% cell
erfi(0.5 + 1.0*im)
#% output
#= 0.18797346722338332 + 0.9507097283189572im

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The derivative:

#% cell
d(erfi(x), x)
#% output
#= 2*exp(x^2)/pi^(1/2)

#% text
# The integral definition:

#% cell
n_integrate(2/sqrt(pi)*exp(t^2), [t, 0, 1]) - erfi(1.0)
#% output
#= 4.440892098500626e-16

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `erf`, `erfc`, `dawson_f`, `fresnel_s`
